  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "Steven Levitsky and Daniel Ziblatt attribute democratic breakdown to the failure of established elites to act as gatekeepers against authoritarian contenders.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "Ronald Inglehart and Pippa Norris argued that support for populist parties reflects cultural backlash more than economic insecurity.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "David Waldner and Ellen Lust published a review of the backsliding literature titled \"Unwelcome Change\" in the Annual Review of Political Science in 2018.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "Waldner and Lust found robust cross-national support for economic inequality as the primary driver of backsliding.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "Process tracing is used by comparativists to test whether a hypothesised causal mechanism operated in a single case.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "Scholars use the sequencing of institutional changes relative to economic shocks as evidence for or against structural explanations.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "pos": 7,
      "text": "Paired comparison of countries with similar economic trajectories but divergent regime outcomes is used to isolate the role of elite choice.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "pos": 8,
      "text": "Poland's Law and Justice party won the 2015 parliamentary election with more than 60 percent of the popular vote.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "pos": 9,
      "text": "Poland's Law and Justice government altered the composition and procedures of the Constitutional Tribunal after taking office in 2015.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "pos": 10,
      "text": "Hungary's electoral law was rewritten in ways that advantaged the incumbent governing party.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "pos": 11,
      "text": "Hungary's public broadcasting apparatus came under the control of the governing party.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "pos": 12,
      "text": "Hungary's 2011 media law established a regulatory council whose members were appointed by the governing majority.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "pos": 13,
      "text": "Nancy Bermeo's 2016 article in the Journal of Democracy argues that executive aggrandizement has replaced the classic coup as the dominant mode of democratic erosion.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "pos": 14,
      "text": "The European Parliament voted in September 2018 to initiate Article 7 proceedings concerning the rule of law in Hungary.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    }
  ],
