  "response": "Adjudication between structural and agency-centred accounts of democratic erosion rarely turns on a single decisive statistical test. It proceeds instead by specifying what each account predicts about timing, about the identity of the actors who move first, and about variation across comparable settings, and then asking which of those predictions the historical record will bear.\n\nSteven Levitsky and Daniel Ziblatt attribute democratic breakdown to the failure of established elites to act as gatekeepers against authoritarian contenders. Ronald Inglehart and Pippa Norris argued that support for populist parties reflects cultural backlash more than economic insecurity. David Waldner and Ellen Lust published a review of the backsliding literature titled \"Unwelcome Change\" in the Annual Review of Political Science in 2018. Whereas Levitsky and Ziblatt place the decisive weight on what party leaders do or fail to do at the gate, Waldner and Lust found robust cross-national support for economic inequality as the primary driver of backsliding; and that cross-national verdict on inequality cannot be squared with the Inglehart and Norris conclusion that cultural rather than material insecurity animates the populist vote, for the two accounts of what moves those voters cannot both be true.\n\nProcess tracing is used by comparativists to test whether a hypothesised causal mechanism operated in a single case. Scholars use the sequencing of institutional changes relative to economic shocks as evidence for or against structural explanations. By contrast with process tracing's attention to the interior of one episode, paired comparison of countries with similar economic trajectories but divergent regime outcomes is used to isolate the role of elite choice.\n\nPoland's Law and Justice party won the 2015 parliamentary election with more than 60 percent of the popular vote. After taking office that year, the government altered the composition and procedures of the Constitutional Tribunal, and this reworking of judicial appointment and procedure supplies strong confirmation for Nancy Bermeo's diagnosis of the characteristic form that erosion now takes. In Hungary, at least one of two things happened, and perhaps both: the electoral law was rewritten in ways that advantaged the incumbent governing party, or the public broadcasting apparatus came under the control of the governing party; the statute books put the second of those developments in exact terms. That Hungary's 2011 media law established a regulatory council whose members were appointed by the governing majority confirms the contention advanced in Nancy Bermeo's 2016 article in the Journal of Democracy, that executive aggrandizement has replaced the classic coup as the dominant mode of democratic erosion. The resulting concentration of regulatory authority in partisan hands led the European Parliament to vote in September 2018 to initiate Article 7 proceedings concerning the rule of law in Hungary.",
